Yes. `None` is an ordinary Python value, so you can pass it in `parametrize` like any other, for example to test the missing-input case. Use `pytest.param(..., id="...")` to give the case a readable name.

```python
import pytest

def normalize(name):
    return (name or "anonymous").strip().title()

@pytest.mark.parametrize("raw, expected", [
    ("  ada ", "Ada"),
    (None, "Anonymous"),
    pytest.param("", "Anonymous", id="empty-string"),
])
def test_normalize(raw, expected):
    assert normalize(raw) == expected
```

Test ids look like `test_normalize[None-Anonymous]`, so the `None` case is visible in the report.

`__rmul__` is the reflected multiplication method. For `a * b`, Python first tries `a.__mul__(b)`. If that is missing or returns `NotImplemented`, it tries `b.__rmul__(a)`. It lets your object work on the right side of `*` with built-in types.

```python
class Vec:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y
    def __mul__(self, k):
        return Vec(self.x * k, self.y * k)
    __rmul__ = __mul__            # so that 3 * v works too
    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Vec({self.x}, {self.y})"

v = Vec(1, 2)
print(v * 3)   # Vec(3, 6)
print(3 * v)   # Vec(3, 6)
```

Without `__rmul__`, `3 * v` raises `TypeError`, because `int.__mul__` does not know how to handle `Vec`.

Register allocation decides which program variables live in CPU registers, which are fast but few, and which are spilled to memory. Two variables can share a register if they are never live at the same time. This is modelled as graph coloring: variables are nodes, an edge connects variables that are live together, and colors are registers.

```python
def color(graph, k):
    assignment = {}
    for v in sorted(graph, key=lambda v: -len(graph[v])):
        used = {assignment[n] for n in graph[v] if n in assignment}
        free = [c for c in range(k) if c not in used]
        assignment[v] = free[0] if free else "spill"
    return assignment

graph = {"a": {"b", "c"}, "b": {"a", "c"}, "c": {"a", "b"}, "d": {"a"}}
print(color(graph, 3))  # {'a': 0, 'b': 1, 'c': 2, 'd': 1}
```

Optimal coloring is NP-hard, so compilers use heuristics (Chaitin-Briggs) or linear scan for speed.

Python has no true private attributes, but you can block access by overriding `__getattribute__`, which runs on every attribute read. Delegate to `object.__getattribute__` for the allowed names; calling `self.something` inside it would recurse forever.

```python
class Guarded:
    _hidden = {"secret"}

    def __init__(self):
        self.secret = 42
        self.public = 1

    def __getattribute__(self, name):
        if name in type(self)._hidden:
            raise AttributeError(f"{name} is not accessible")
        return object.__getattribute__(self, name)

g = Guarded()
print(g.public)   # 1
try:
    g.secret
except AttributeError as e:
    print(e)      # secret is not accessible
```

This is only a guard against accidents; anyone can still read `g.__dict__`. A leading underscore is the normal convention for "private".

Use a dict comprehension that applies `int` to each value. Keys stay the same.

```python
d = {"a": "1", "b": "22", "c": "3"}
print({k: int(v) for k, v in d.items()})  # {'a': 1, 'b': 22, 'c': 3}

messy = {"x": "5", "y": "oops"}
clean = {}
for k, v in messy.items():
    try:
        clean[k] = int(v)
    except ValueError:
        pass
print(clean)  # {'x': 5}
```

`int("3.5")` raises `ValueError`, so use `int(float(v))` for decimal strings. The loop version lets you skip or handle bad values.

Bubble sort repeatedly walks through the list, swaps adjacent items that are out of order, and so lets the largest remaining value "bubble" to the end after each pass. It stops when a pass makes no swaps. It is simple but O(n^2), so it is mostly a teaching tool.

```python
def bubble_sort(items):
    items = list(items)
    for end in range(len(items) - 1, 0, -1):
        swapped = False
        for i in range(end):
            if items[i] > items[i + 1]:
                items[i], items[i + 1] = items[i + 1], items[i]
                swapped = True
        if not swapped:
            break
    return items

print(bubble_sort([64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90]))  # [11, 12, 22, 25, 34, 64, 90]
```

Use `sorted()` in real code.

Inverting (mirroring) a binary tree means swapping the left and right child of every node. A recursive function does it in O(n) time, with stack depth equal to the tree height.

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, left=None, right=None):
        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, left, right

def invert(root):
    if root:
        root.left, root.right = invert(root.right), invert(root.left)
    return root

def inorder(n):
    return inorder(n.left) + [n.val] + inorder(n.right) if n else []

t = Node(2, Node(1), Node(3))
print(inorder(t))          # [1, 2, 3]
print(inorder(invert(t)))  # [3, 2, 1]
```

For very deep trees use an explicit stack or queue to avoid hitting the recursion limit.

`enumerate` yields `(index, item)` pairs, and `dict()` accepts pairs, so wrapping it builds an index-to-item mapping. Pass `start=1` to begin at 1, and swap the pair to map items to positions.

```python
letters = ["a", "b", "c"]
print(dict(enumerate(letters)))                  # {0: 'a', 1: 'b', 2: 'c'}
print(dict(enumerate(letters, start=1)))         # {1: 'a', 2: 'b', 3: 'c'}
print({v: i for i, v in enumerate(letters)})     # {'a': 0, 'b': 1, 'c': 2}
```

In the reversed form, duplicates collapse to the last index.

A descriptor is an object with `__get__`, `__set__` or `__delete__` that lives as a class attribute and controls access to that attribute on instances. Properties, methods and `classmethod` are built on this. `__set_name__` tells the descriptor the attribute name it was assigned to.

```python
class Positive:
    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):
        self.name = "_" + name
    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):
        return self if obj is None else getattr(obj, self.name)
    def __set__(self, obj, value):
        if value <= 0:
            raise ValueError("must be positive")
        setattr(obj, self.name, value)

class Item:
    price = Positive()
    def __init__(self, price):
        self.price = price

i = Item(5)
print(i.price)          # 5
try:
    Item(-1)
except ValueError as e:
    print(e)            # must be positive
```

Use a descriptor to share validation logic between many attributes.

A regular method receives the instance (`self`). A `@classmethod` receives the class (`cls`), which is useful for alternative constructors and works correctly with subclasses. A `@staticmethod` receives nothing extra: it is just a function placed in the class namespace.

```python
class Date:
    def __init__(self, y, m, d):
        self.y, self.m, self.d = y, m, d

    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, text):
        return cls(*map(int, text.split("-")))

    @staticmethod
    def is_leap(year):
        return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)

d = Date.from_string("2026-05-14")
print(d.y, d.m, d.d)        # 2026 5 14
print(Date.is_leap(2024))   # True
```

If a method needs neither the instance nor the class, a plain module-level function is often better.

Implement `__getitem__`. When someone writes `obj[1:3]`, Python passes a `slice` object, so check for it and delegate to the underlying sequence. Integer indexing passes an `int` to the same method.

```python
class Playlist:
    def __init__(self, songs):
        self._songs = list(songs)

    def __getitem__(self, index):
        if isinstance(index, slice):
            return Playlist(self._songs[index])
        return self._songs[index]

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self._songs)

p = Playlist(["a", "b", "c", "d"])
print(p[1])            # b
print(p[1:3]._songs)   # ['b', 'c']
print(len(p[::2]))     # 2
```

Defining `__getitem__` and `__len__` also makes the object iterable and reversible.

Put several exception types in a tuple in a single `except`, or use separate `except` clauses when each needs different handling. Python checks them from top to bottom and runs the first match.

```python
def ratio(a, b):
    try:
        return int(a) / int(b)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return "bad input"
    except ZeroDivisionError:
        return "divide by zero"

print(ratio("10", "4"))   # 2.5
print(ratio("x", "4"))    # bad input
print(ratio("1", "0"))    # divide by zero
```

